---
description: Apply when creating commits and PRs, preparing work for review, or responding to review
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- A PR is an argument that a change is safe to merge; optimize how cheaply the reviewer reconstructs and checks your reasoning. Past ~400 lines of judgment code, defect-finding collapses.
- One concern per PR: a feature, a fix, or a refactor — never mixed with drive-by renames or reformatting. Splits (and merge order): refactor-then-behave · mechanical-then-manual (the codemod apart from the judgment lines) · expand-then-contract for migrations. A summary needing "also" is two PRs. Label generated files and lockfiles.
- Commits as narrative: each one a buildable logical step (bisect lands on it); subject imperative; body = why (the constraint, the bug link, the rejected alternative) — the diff already shows what. Squash wip/fix-typo noise before review.
- Description front-loads: the problem before the solution (issue linked) · the approach + the rejected alternative · risk & proof — tests added and verification run, output quoted (the most-skipped, highest-value section) · a guided tour when the diff is nonlinear. Then self-review your own full diff as the reviewer and run the suite.
- Review dialogue: every comment answered — fixed (with commit) / pushback (with reasoning) / deferred (with ticket link). No force-push over an in-progress review; response commits separate from new feature work. Reviewing: approach before mechanics, blocking vs preference marked, turnaround treated as production work.
- Merge on green CI with resolved threads; squash-merge means the PR title/description become the commit — write them like it. Delete the branch. Never self-merge "trivial" changes without review.
